### 2. War Stories Extracted

**War Story 1 (Amon) — The context window cost shock**
- Setup: Amon was designing or advising on an AI system, moving from a hosted solution (ChatGPT/Claude interface) to building the inference layer directly.
- Pressure: Had to figure out what the model actually needs to produce one accurate answer.
- Decision/realization: "It's so much data you have to send to make one right decision." The entire right-sizing calculation changes once you see the full context payload required per request.
- Outcome: "This really changes your all the right sizing calculation, everything you have thought about — it's not like you ask it and it will just answer it, so it's not a one-way sheet. You have to give lot of things to it to just get one accurate answer."
- Atul corroborates from personal experience: "It's only when you actually get out from a hosted solution to building something on your own that's when you realize the entire context window aspect of it... every time that chunk has to go, plus keep adding on top of it."
- **Narrative gap:** No specific numbers, no client name, no outcome metric. The emotional landing (calculation surprise) is clear but the story trails off.

**War Story 2 (Amon) — GPU infrastructure assumptions upended overnight**
- Setup: Teams planning AI product infrastructure around existing GPU availability and pricing.
- Pressure: A major market event (almost certainly DeepSeek R1, described as "last 10 days when the world turned into the red") disrupted assumptions about what compute would be required and at what cost.
- Decision: "We still don't know whether this is certain or not how much... whatever is out there you still need a lot to run models."
- Outcome: Right-sizing is not a one-time decision. The AI compute landscape can shift fast enough that infrastructure assumptions made six months ago may be wrong today.
- **Narrative gap:** The market event is alluded to but not named. The specific impact on Amon's team or clients is not stated.

**War Story 3 (Amon) — Network bandwidth as a surprise bottleneck**
- Setup: Teams designing GPU clusters for AI inference, experienced in traditional compute (10Gbps LAN).
- Pressure: Inference parallelism requires shuffling large amounts of data between machines — pipeline parallelism, tensor parallelism.
- Decision: "You cannot sustain with 10Gbps. You might need like better 200Gbps lines, you need smarter switches that do network-level offloading."
- Outcome: Infrastructure teams discover this gap only when models underperform or GPUs are underutilized. The problem is invisible until you measure it.
- **Narrative gap:** No specific incident or client scenario — this reads as pattern observation rather than a "this actually happened" story. Worth flagging for follow-up.

**War Story 4 (Amon/Craig, collaborative exchange) — Circuit breaker in production: Claude's own overload message**
- Setup: Craig raises the pattern of model fallback when a primary model goes down or is version-updated.
- Amon builds: "The classic example — if you have been using Claude, active one time a day you will definitely see a message that we are overbooked right now, switching your all the requests to Sonnet or Opus. They do that — even at running at such a good scale, having such a good infra, they also get overloaded."
- Pressure: A product's user base can spike from 100 to 1,000 during an event. You may not have load-tested at that scale. The primary model cannot handle it.
- Decision: Circuit breaker routes to a lower-capability model that "can still give responses, not so many capabilities but still we'll handle that — we have designed the system for that."
- Outcome: Observation-level (Anthropic does this), not a specific client outcome from Amon's team. **Narrative gap:** No specifics from a real deployment Amon or Craig personally worked on.

**War Story 5 (Craig) — Model version update breaks application code**
- Setup: Developer pins to a model version, deploys to production.
- Pressure: Provider updates the model without the developer's control.
- Decision: "You've moved your problem there... the way that you might be expecting things to work can immediately change if you don't have that pinned."
- Outcome: Code behavior changes silently in production. Craig presents this as a real pattern he has observed, not as a specific incident. **Narrative gap:** No concrete failure case or recovery story.

